{
  "slug": "artist:bobby-brown",
  "name": "Bobby Brown",
  "editorial_article": "Born in 1969 in the Orchard Park Projects of Boston, Bobby Brown ascended from local talent shows to become a defining architect of new jack swing alongside producer Teddy Riley. After co-leading New Edition to 1983 chart success with the Streetwise Records hit 'Candy Girl', he departed for a solo career that broke Billboard 200 records. His 1988 sophomore album 'Don't Be Cruel' made him the youngest male artist to top the Billboard 200 with a studio album at age 19, eventually shipping over 12 million units worldwide. The project yielded five top-ten Hot 100 singles, including the Grammy-winning 'Every Little Step' and the self-penned anthem 'My Prerogative'. Beyond his musical output, Brown pioneered stage mannerisms like the 'Roger Rabbit' dance and maintained a long-term professional association with New Edition, participating in the group's 1996 'Home Again' reunion and their 2022 Culture Tour.",
